\foreach \y in {0.4,0.8,1.2,1.6,2.4,2.8,3.2,3.6,4.4,4.8,5.2,5.6} {\draw (-0.1,\y)--(0,\y);}
\foreach \y/\lbl in {0/0,2/50,4/100,6/150} {\draw (-0.18,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\foreach \x/\lbl in {1/2021,2.4/2022,3.8/2023,5.2/2024,6.6/2025} {\node[below,font=\tiny] at (\x,0) {\lbl};}
\draw[thick] (1,2.4)--(2.4,4.0)--(3.8,3.6)--(5.2,6.0)--(6.6,5.2);
\foreach \p in {(1,2.4),(2.4,4.0),(3.8,3.6),(5.2,6.0),(6.6,5.2)} {\fill \p circle (2.5pt);}
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-1.9,3.0) {Entries};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.8,-1.0) {Year};
\node[font=\small] at (3.8,7.1) {Entries each year};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The line graph shows how many students entered events at a school's sports carnival in each of five years. A new house-points system was introduced in $2023$. A teacher writes three conclusions:
**1** Entries in $2025$ were more than double the entries in $2021$.
**2** The house-points system caused the rise in entries.
**3** Entries will be higher again in $2026$.
Decide whether each conclusion is supported, and explain your decision.
